8,54934,16,11,15,31870,87,12445,7,17,4806,4439,18552,4999,18,11,18,701,1151,626,1502,35963,2577,58,16,1125,330,32,497,320,15,4999,16,15,1106,1502,35963,2577,58,17,1125,330,33,497,320,15,4999,16,15,1106,1502,35963,2577,58,18,1125,330,34,497,320,15,4999,16,15,1106,1502,35963,2577,58,19,1125,330,35,497,320,15,4999,16,15,1106,1502,35963,2577,58,20,1125,330,36,497,320,15,4999,16,15,3237,718,35963,2577,58,16,2558,718,90278,1188,17,15,11,15,83738,76039,58,17,14382,718,90278,1188,19,15,11,15,83738,76039,58,18,14382,718,90278,1188,16,15,4999,17,15,83738,76039,58,19,14382,718,90278,1188,18,15,4999,17,15,83738,76039,58,20,14382,24157,6405,2533,6269,279,6524,315,279,4771,315,400,88,284,282,7,17,87,488,220,16,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\nreal func(real x) {\n  real y;\n  if (x >= -3 && x <= 0) {y = -2 - x;}\n  if (x >= 0 && x <= 2) {y = sqrt(4 - (x - 2)^2) - 2;}\n  if (x >= 2 && x <= 3) {y = 2*(x - 2);}\n  return(y);\n}\n\nint i, n;\n\nfor (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n  draw((i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n  draw((-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n}\n\ndraw((-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\ndraw((0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\nlabel(\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n\ndraw(graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(\"$y = f(x)$\", (4,-3), UnFill);\n[/asy]\n\nWhich is the graph of $y = f(2x + 1)$?\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\npicture[] graf;\nint i, n;\n\nreal func(real x) {\n  real y;\n  if (x >= -3 && x <= 0) {y = -2 - x;}\n  if (x >= 0 && x <= 2) {y = sqrt(4 - (x - 2)^2) - 2;}\n  if (x >= 2 && x <= 3) {y = 2*(x - 2);}\n  return(y);\n}\n\nreal funca(real x) {\n  return(func(2*x + 1));\n}\n\nfor (n = 1; n <= 5; ++n) {\n  graf[n] = new picture;\n  for (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n    draw(graf[n],(i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n    draw(graf[n],(-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n  }\n  draw(graf[n],(-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\n  draw(graf[n],(0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\n  label(graf[n],\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\n  label(graf[n],\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n}\n\ndraw(graf[1],graph(funca,-2,1),red);\ndraw(graf[2],shift((1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[3],shift((1/2,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[4],shift((-1,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[5],shift((-1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(graf[1], \"A\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[2], \"B\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[3], \"C\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[4], \"D\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[5], \"E\", (0,-10));\n\nadd(graf[1]);\nadd(shift((20,0))*(graf[2]));\nadd(shift((40,0))*(graf[3]));\nadd(shift((10,-20))*(graf[4]));\nadd(shift((30,-20))*(graf[5]));\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letter of the graph of $y = f(2x + 1).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b84643a014ad5cd0a125540eb0c05a5d1eb76f04d22980990a38d175512898dd"},"seconds":33.827340602874756,"seed":596539173816703976,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of finding which graph represents $y = f(2x + 1)$, we need to understand how the transformation $2x + 1$ affects the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n1. **Horizontal Compression by a Factor of 2**: The multiplication by 2 inside the function argument causes a horizontal compression by a factor of 2. This means that each point $(x, y)$ on the graph of $y = f(x)$ will be transformed to a point $\\left(\\frac{x}{2}, y\\right)$ on the graph of $y = f(2x)$.\n\n2. **Horizontal Shift to the Left by 1/2**: The addition of 1 inside the function argument, $2x + 1$, causes a horizontal shift. Specifically, it shifts the graph to the left by $\\frac{1}{2}$ units. This is because the transformation $2x + 1$ can be rewritten as $2(x + \\frac{1}{2})$, indicating a shift of $-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction.\n\nGiven these transformations, let's analyze the provided graphs:\n\n- **Graph A**: This graph shows a horizontally compressed version of $y = f(x)$ and shifted to the left, which aligns with the transformations for $y = f(2x + 1)$. Hence, it is a likely candidate.\n- **Graph B**: This graph shows a horizontally stretched version (by a factor of 2) and shifted to the right, which does not align with the transformations.\n- **Graph C**: This graph shows a horizontally compressed version (by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$) and shifted to the right, which does not align with the transformations.\n- **Graph D**: This graph shows a horizontally compressed version and shifted to the left, similar to Graph A, but the details may differ.\n- **Graph E**: This graph shows a horizontally stretched version and shifted to the left, which does not align with the transformations.\n\nUpon comparing these descriptions to the transformations needed for $y = f(2x + 1)$, we find that Graph A correctly represents the graph after applying a horizontal compression by a factor of 2 and a horizontal shift to the left by $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph of $y = f(2x + 1)$ is represented by \\(\\boxed{A}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{A}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
